<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    API 31 and above. `backup_rules.xml` says the same thing for 26 to 30, which
    is why the two exist; keep them in step.

    Android splits "copy this to the cloud" from "copy this to the user's next
    phone", and this app wants opposite answers to those two questions.

    A custom pictogram is a photograph of somebody's kitchen, their bus stop,
    their grandmother, and the database next to it is every word they can say.
    That is not something to put in a Drive backup on the app's initiative and
    without telling anybody — and the privacy policy and the store listing both
    promise it stays on the device.

    Phone-to-phone transfer is a different act with a different destination. It
    is the user moving their own data to their own next phone, in one motion,
    directly, at the moment they choose. Turning it off alongside the cloud would
    cost the population this app is for the most: a board is hours of a
    caregiver's work, and losing it when a handset breaks costs somebody their
    voice.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <!--
        Every domain the app writes to, named individually because there is no
        wildcard and an unlisted domain is an included one. `external` is listed
        despite the app not using external storage, so that starting to use it
        cannot quietly opt those files in.
    -->
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
    </cloud-backup>

    <!--
        Deliberately empty: no rules means everything transfers. The element is
        written out rather than omitted so that the asymmetry above reads as a
        decision instead of a gap somebody later fills in.
    -->
    <device-transfer />
</data-extraction-rules>
